Task: Test runner entry point (Phase 1, Track foundation-core_20260918)

What was done:
- Wrote a runner that discovers every test file under the core test directory,
  loads it, registers what it returns, and runs the collected tests.
- It reports how many files it found and how many tests passed or failed, and
  exits with a status that is zero only when everything passed.
- Verified all three behaviors with a temporary test file rather than assuming
  them: a deliberately failing test produced a failure count, a pointer to the
  offending file and a non-zero exit; the same test made to pass produced a
  success count and a zero exit; with no test files present the runner reports
  zero and exits clean. The temporary file was removed afterwards.

Findings worth keeping:
- Compiled modules from the package manager land in a directory whose name
  differs between distributions on 64-bit systems. The runner offers both
  rather than guessing, which is why it works here without the caller
  preparing anything.
- The directory-listing library arrives as a dependency of the linter, but it
  is now installed explicitly in the setup notes, because depending on another
  tool's dependency is a trap for the next machine.

Files changed:
- tests/run_all.lua, conductor/workflow.md

Why:
Test-driven work needs a red phase that can be seen. Until a runner reports
failure distinctly from success, 'write a failing test first' is an
instruction nobody can follow.
